Emperor penguins are the champions of depth. They have been recorded diving to about 560 m, about two-thirds of the height of the [[@extreme-tallest-structures|Burj Khalifa]]. The longest recorded dive, measured in 2018, lasted about 32 minutes.

How do they hold their breath so long? They store oxygen in their blood and muscles, slow their heartbeat, and send blood only to the parts that need it most. Their muscles are packed with a protein that holds oxygen, which makes penguin meat very dark red.

{{story:wild-penguin-dads}}

A famous 2015 study estimated that about 8 million tonnes of plastic reached the sea from land in a single year, in 2010. Other estimates are higher or lower, because it is very hard to measure. But when scientists add up all the plastic they can find floating, it is only a small part of what should be there.

So where is the rest? Some has probably sunk, carried down by its own weight or inside the droppings of animals that ate it. Some is buried in beaches and coastal mud. Some has broken into pieces too small to catch in nets. In 2018, Japanese scientists reported a plastic bag spotted at the bottom of the Mariana Trench, almost 11 km down, near the [[@ocean-deepest|deepest point in the ocean]].

{{story:recycling}}

There's another reason tigers aren't green. Almost no mammal can make green fur. Mammal hair gets its colour from just two pigments, one making black and brown, the other making orange and yellow. Mix them in different amounts and you get the colours of tigers, lions, dogs and people. [[@power-sloth|Sloths]] look greenish only because tiny algae grow in their fur.

For a hunter whose main prey can't see red, orange works perfectly well. It may even be the best a mammal can do.

{{story:octopus-smart}}

Around 2012, a young mountain lion crossed two of the busiest freeways in [[@los-angeles|Los Angeles]] and settled in Griffith Park, a large urban park near the Hollywood sign. Scientists called him P-22 and fitted him with a tracking collar. He lived there for about ten years, often caught on hidden cameras.

P-22 became a local celebrity. His story helped win support for a wildlife bridge over a busy freeway near Los Angeles, designed so mountain lions and other animals can cross safely. He died in 2022, old and injured, but his story changed how many people saw their city.

{{story:pigeons-homing}}

In 1876, the German engineer Nikolaus Otto built a working four-stroke engine. Others turned it into transport. In 1886, Karl Benz patented a three-wheeled car with a small petrol engine. People weren't sure it was practical.

In August 1888, his wife, Bertha Benz, settled the question. Without telling her husband, she drove the car about 100 kilometres from Mannheim to her mother's home in Pforzheim with their two teenage sons. She bought fuel from a chemist's shop and fixed problems along the way. The trip showed the car could really be used, and the route is now a tourist trail.

{{story:cars-history}}

During the [[@world-war-one|First World War]], soldiers lived in long ditches called trenches. Raising your head above the edge was very dangerous. Soldiers used simple box periscopes to watch the land in front without showing themselves. Some were made from wood and cheap mirrors. Others were long, folding brass tubes.

Some inventors fitted periscopes to rifles, so a soldier could aim without lifting his head. These trench periscopes are now in museums such as the Imperial War Museums in Britain.

{{story:submarines}}

Most touch lamps have more than two settings. Each touch tells a tiny chip to move to the next step: dim, medium, bright, then off. The chip doesn't make the bulb weaker. Instead it switches the power on and off very fast, many times a second. The longer each "on" moment lasts, the brighter the [[@light-bulb|light bulb]] looks. Your eyes blend the flicker into a steady glow.

Because the lamp is always sensing, it can sometimes switch on by itself. Electrical noise from a storm, a power surge or a nearby motor can fool the circuit into thinking someone touched it. Some lamps come with a small add-on to filter out that noise.

{{story:touchscreens}}

Some colour rules that feel ancient are surprisingly young. In the early 1900s, American babies were mostly dressed in white, which could be boiled and bleached clean. When pastel colours became fashionable, there was no fixed rule. In 1918, a trade magazine for baby-clothes shops wrote that pink, "being a more decided and stronger color", suited boys, while blue, "more delicate and dainty", suited girls.

Advice went both ways for years. Only around the 1940s and 1950s did "pink for girls, blue for boys" settle in the USA, helped along by shops and advertisers. The historian Jo Paoletti traced this story through old catalogues, magazines and photographs.

{{story:holi}}

Dried clay is still fragile. Drop it in water and it will slowly fall apart. The big change happens in the kiln.

Above about 600 °C, water locked inside the clay's crystals is driven out. At higher temperatures, parts of the clay begin to melt and fuse. Everyday earthenware, like a red [[@terracotta|terracotta]] pot, is fired at about 1,000 °C. Stoneware and porcelain are fired hotter, around 1,200 to 1,400 °C, which makes them denser and stronger. A glaze is a coating of fine powder that melts in the kiln into a thin layer of [[@glass-made|glass]].

{{story:wheel-invented}}

On Dal Lake in Srinagar, a post office floats on a houseboat. It sells stamps, collects letters from people living around the lake, and has a small museum of stamps. Many visitors post a card from it just to say they did.

In the high Himalaya, the far Northeast and the islands of the Andamans, the post travels by everything from ferries to helicopters. Before modern roads, India relied on relay runners, called dak runners. Each ran a fixed stretch, then handed the bag on, like a relay race across the country.

{{story:oddity-floating-post-office}}

In 1983, on a TV special, Jackson performed "Billie Jean" and suddenly glided backwards while seeming to walk forwards. Viewers were stunned. The step, which he called the moonwalk, depends on smooth shoes and careful control of [[@friction|friction]]: one foot slides while the other grips.

{{story:friction}}

He kept inventing. For the "Smooth Criminal" lean, he helped design shoes that hooked onto pegs in the stage floor. He received a US [[@patents|patent]] for them in 1993.

Indian boards were often hand-painted on cloth or paper, with gods, animals and labels in [[@sanskrit|Sanskrit]], Hindi, Marathi or other languages. Squares had names such as faith, generosity or knowledge at the foot of ladders. Anger, theft, pride and drunkenness sat at the snakes' heads.

The idea fitted beliefs shared by Hindus, Jains and Buddhists about karma, the idea that actions have consequences across lifetimes. Jain boards, sometimes called gyanbazi, have their own special squares and symbols.

{{story:snakes-venom}}

In the 1760s, a [[@scotland|Scottish instrument maker]] named James Watt was asked to repair a model Newcomen engine. He noticed how much fuel it wasted. Each stroke, the cylinder was cooled to turn the steam back into water, then heated again.

Watt's answer was a separate condenser, a chamber where the steam could cool while the main cylinder stayed hot. He patented it in 1769. Later he and his business partner Matthew Boulton added ways to make the engine turn wheels smoothly. Now steam could drive [[@clothes-cotton|cotton]] mills, flour mills and ironworks. The unit of power, the watt, is named after him.

{{story:pressure-cooker}}

Nobody can make a single, perfect slab of glass 39 m wide, and no road could carry it. So the ELT's main mirror is made of 798 hexagonal segments that fit together like tiles. Motors behind each one adjust it many times a second, keeping the whole surface in shape as the wind blows and the temperature changes.

The Giant Magellan Telescope takes a different path. It will use seven round mirrors, each 8.4 m across, arranged like the petals of a flower. Together they act like one mirror about 25 m wide. Each mirror is cast in a spinning oven at the University of Arizona, in a lab under the stands of the university's football stadium. A mirror takes years to cast, cool and polish.

{{story:exoplanets}}

There was not enough dry land to feed the city, so the Mexica made more. They built chinampas: long, narrow plots raised from layers of lake mud and rotting plants, held together with posts and willow roots. Maize, beans, squash, [[@chillies|chillies]] and flowers grew on them.

Canals between the plots worked like streets. Farmers paddled canoes to market, a little like the boatmen of [[@venice|Venice]]. At Xochimilco, in the south of Mexico City, some chinampas are still farmed, and families ride colourful flat boats along the old canals on weekends.

{{story:venice}}

British rules made large political meetings risky. Tilak found another way to gather people. From 1893, he encouraged public celebrations of [[@ganesh-chaturthi|Ganesh Chaturthi]], which had mostly been a family festival at home. Neighbourhoods set up big Ganesh idols, held processions and organised talks and songs, some of them about the nation.

He also promoted a festival honouring the Maratha king Shivaji. These events built pride and drew huge crowds. Some historians note that festivals linked to one community could make others feel left out, and the question is still debated.

{{story:ganesh-chaturthi}}

Each big temple sits on a high stone platform. Above the main shrine rises a curved tower called a *shikhara*, and around it smaller towers climb up like foothills. The idea was to copy Mount Kailash, the home of Shiva in the Himalaya. From a distance, the Kandariya Mahadeva temple looks like a cluster of peaks about 31 metres tall, roughly the height of a ten-storey building.

The builders used buff and pink sandstone from quarries near the Ken river. Blocks were shaped to fit tightly together, and the weight of the stone holds much of the building in place.

{{story:architecture-temples}}

Kedarnath, a temple to Shiva, stands at about 3,580 m, near the source of the Mandakini river. Badrinath, a temple to Vishnu, sits beside the Alaknanda. Both close in winter, when snow blocks the paths, and reopen in spring. Many pilgrims still walk the last stretch on foot. The path to Kedarnath climbs about 16 km from the nearest road, and ponies, porters and helicopters help those who can't make the climb.

Rishikesh, on the Ganga near the foothills, is known as a centre for [[@ayurveda-yoga|yoga]] and [[@mindfulness|meditation]]. In 1968, the [[@beatles-music|Beatles]] stayed at an ashram there, and many songs on their "White Album" were written during that visit. Today, people from all over the world come to study yoga beside the river.

{{story:chipko}}

In 1881, Robert Louis Stevenson was stuck indoors on a rainy holiday in Scotland. He painted a map of an island with his 13-year-old stepson, Lloyd Osbourne. Looking at it, he started imagining the [[@pirates|pirates]] who had been there. That became Treasure Island, published as a book in 1883.

It follows young Jim Hawkins, a one-legged pirate named Long John Silver and a hunt for buried gold. Much of what people "know" about pirates, from treasure maps to parrots on shoulders, comes from this book.

{{story:pirates}}

The Portuguese reached India in 1498 and took over Goa in 1510. Their ships brought several American crops, including chillies, [[@cashew|cashews]] and [[@guava-papaya|papaya]]. Farmers found that chillies grew quickly, produced lots of fruit, and could be dried and stored for a year.

A plant that gave you pepper-like heat for almost nothing was hard to resist. Chillies spread inland along trade routes. By the 1600s they were growing in many parts of India. Today India grows more chillies than any other country, and Guntur in Andhra Pradesh has one of the largest chilli markets in Asia.

{{story:goa}}
